# 20FIT CRM — environment variables
# NAMES ONLY. Never put real values here. Real values are set in Railway
# (Variables tab) and, for local dev, in .env.local (git-ignored).

# --- Supabase: project "20FIT ALL DATA" (cpvzwqptzcxnwzfzgrmt) ---
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# SERVER ONLY. Bypasses all RLS — the most sensitive credential in the system.
# Never prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_ (that would broadcast it to the browser).
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- App ---
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_ENV — currently unused (Sprint 1), intentionally left with NO
# value. Railway's "Suggested Variables" reads values straight from source files,
# so a hard-coded `development` here is the same footgun that poisoned a build via
# NODE_ENV. If this is reintroduced later, set it in Railway (production) and
# .env.local (development) — never hard-code a value in this template.
#
# NODE_ENV is managed automatically and must NOT be set here (or in .env.local).
# Next.js sets it: production for `next build` / `next start`, development for
# `next dev`; Railway builds as production. A stray `development` value at build
# time poisons the production build — React's dev and prod runtimes get mixed and
# every page throws "Cannot read properties of null (reading 'useContext')".

# --- AI segment assistant (Sprint 4A) — SERVER ONLY ---
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: the LLM that maps free text to segment criteria. Server-side only; never
# prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_. If unset, the AI assistant 503s and the manual filter builder still
# works (AI accelerates, it does not replace). SEGMENT_AI_MODEL overrides the default model id.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# SEGMENT_AI_MODEL=

# --- Not used in Sprint 1 (listed so they are not forgotten later) ---
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
# WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# WHATSAPP_WEBHOOK_VERIFY_TOKEN=
# MAYAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# --- Sending a campaign / internal send test (ALL required before ANY email goes out) ---
# The send path fail-closes if any of these are missing — and it does so BEFORE the first row, so a
# missing one used to fail silently (T-30). The pre-check now reports every missing one at once.
#   UNSUBSCRIBE_TOKEN_SECRET — REQUIRED, >=16 chars. HMACs crm_message_log.identity_hash AND signs
#     unsubscribe links (one secret, two domain-separated uses). Absent => send throws at the secret
#     read, before any row. This was the cause of the first internal-test failure.
#   MAILTRAP_API_TOKEN, MAILTRAP_FROM — REQUIRED to authenticate + set the From address (already set).
# UNSUBSCRIBE_TOKEN_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-string
#
# MAILTRAP_WEBHOOK_SECRET — needed by the Mailtrap webhook (delivered/bounce cycle stamps); absent =>
#   the webhook 401s. Not required for the send itself, but the next thing to break.
# MAILTRAP_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL — base for unsubscribe links; falls back to https://crm.20fit.id. Confirm it
#   points at the real CRM URL, else unsubscribe links go to the wrong place.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://crm.20fit.id
#
# CAMPAIGN_SEND_ENABLED — LEAVE UNSET until the token is rotated AND SPF/DKIM/DMARC are in place.
#   Absent / "false" / "0" all mean OFF (safe: only @20fit.id addresses go out, customers withheld).
#   Only the exact string "true" turns real customer sending on.
# CAMPAIGN_SEND_ENABLED=
#
# The single @20fit.id destination the pre-launch send-test harness sends to. NOT hardcoded (an
# address in code would ship in the deploy forever). The harness refuses if unset, if the address is
# not @20fit.id, or if CAMPAIGN_SEND_ENABLED is true (it runs only in safe mode — never a backdoor).
# SEND_TEST_INTERNAL_ADDRESS=you@20fit.id
